# Backend JavaScript Coding Standards

## ES6 Modules and Imports
- Always use ES6 `import/export` syntax (project uses `"type": "module"`)
- Use default exports for main classes: `export default ClassName`
- Import with explicit `.js` extensions: `import ChatService from './chatService.js'`

## Class Structure
- Use ES6 classes for all service and client implementations
- Constructor should initialize all dependencies
- Methods should be async when dealing with external APIs
- Always include error handling with try-catch blocks

## Error Handling Pattern
```javascript
try {
    const result = await someAsyncOperation();
    return result;
} catch (error) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to process operation: ${error.message}`);
}
```

## Dependency Injection
- Inject dependencies through constructor parameters
- Use dependency injection for testability
- Example from [chatService.js](mdc:backend/src/application/chatService.js):
```javascript
constructor() {
    this.ollamaClient = new OllamaClient();
    this.chatHistory = new ChatHistory();
    this.seleniumService = new SeleniumService();
}
```

## API Response Patterns
- Use consistent response structure for SSE endpoints
- Include `type` field for message classification: `'ai'`, `'human'`, `'system'`, `'error'`
- Always set proper SSE headers:
```javascript
res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');
```

## Async/Await Usage
- Prefer async/await over Promises
- Use `for await` loops for streaming responses
- Handle cleanup in async methods (e.g., `cleanup()` method)

## File Naming Conventions
- Use camelCase for file names: `chatService.js`, `ollamaClient.js`
- Match class names with file names
- Use descriptive names that reflect functionality